{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim of 'performative contradiction' fails because it conflates Popper's methodological rule for choosing hypotheses to test with an inductive assertion of their future truth. The target ignores the specific countercondition that a preference can be justified solely as a strategy to maximize the severity of future tests (falsifiability), not as a prediction of success. By asserting that acting on corroboration 'implicitly asserts... future stability,' the target attributes a probabilistic belief to the agent that Popper explicitly denies; one can choose the best-tested theory to act upon precisely because it is the most vulnerable to refutation, thereby advancing knowledge regardless of whether the action 'succeeds' in a pragmatic sense. The target offers no argument against this non-inductive, critical rationale, rendering its conclusion that the preference 'smuggles in induction' unsupported.","premise":"Acting on the best-corroborated theory can be justified methodologically as selecting the hypothesis that offers the greatest opportunity for severe testing, without assuming it will remain unfalsified.","premises_essential":["f1e8ebb94f685802462f30e69112a7b386ff0f0aac65087f14bca238fc72b427"],"successor_question":"Does the target provide any textual evidence from Popper suggesting he viewed the choice of a well-corroborated theory for action as an endorsement of its future reliability rather than a strategic move to expose it to further risk?","target":"f1e8ebb94f685802462f30e69112a7b386ff0f0aac65087f14bca238fc72b427"}]}